  "content": "<p>Check your understanding of this lesson. Your answers aren't recorded.</p>\n<multiple-choice question=\"What is texel density?\" single-option=\"\" randomize=\"\">\n  <input type=\"checkbox\" value=\"The number of texture pixels per unit of surface on a model\" data-correct=\"true\">\n  <input type=\"checkbox\" value=\"The number of polygons per object\">\n  <input type=\"checkbox\" value=\"The file size of a texture\">\n  <input type=\"checkbox\" value=\"The number of materials in a scene\">\n</multiple-choice>\n<true-false-question question=\"Combining several objects' textures into one atlas with a shared material can reduce draw calls.\">\n  <input type=\"checkbox\" value=\"True\" data-correct=\"true\">\n  <input type=\"checkbox\" value=\"False\">\n</true-false-question>\n<self-check title=\"Think it through\">\n  <p slot=\"question\">Why do real-time assets need tighter budgets than assets for an offline render?</p>\n  <p>A game has to draw each frame in a few milliseconds (about 16 ms at 60 fps) on players' hardware with limited memory, while an offline render can spend minutes per frame on a render farm. So polygon counts, texture memory and draw calls all have to be limited.</p>\n</self-check>\n"
}